# Test: the daemon refuses to start on a config a registered validator rejects.
#
# VALIDATES: spec-fixit-config-validators-bypassed-at-startup AC-1 -- a value a
#            ze:validate custom validator refuses stops `ze` at load. The daemon
#            exits 1, names the section, the leaf and the rule, and binds
#            nothing, so the value reaches no protocol encoder.
# PREVENTS:  the regression this spec closes. Until 2026-08-11 LoadConfig parsed
#            and returned, and ValidateTreeAllModules had exactly one non-test
#            caller (`ze config validate`), so every custom validator was
#            bypassed at daemon start and at SIGHUP. A hand-edited config
#            reached the wire unvalidated.
#
# The value below passes the YANG schema on its own: the `use` leaf is
# `type string { length "1..64" }`, so nothing but the custom validator can
# refuse it. That is what makes this test discriminate -- revert the
# ValidateCustomSections call in LoadConfig and seq 1 stops exiting at all: the
# daemon starts, logs "unknown internal plugin: p1" from plugin.server, and runs
# until the timeout kills it.
#
# The enforcing producer is InternalPluginNameValidator
# (internal/component/config/validators.go), reached through the ze:validate
# extension on plugin/internal/use in
# internal/component/plugin/yang/ze-plugin-conf.yang, walked by
# ValidateCustomSections (internal/component/config/validate_sections.go).
#
# TWO SHAPES BELOW ARE LOAD-BEARING, and neither is about BGP.
#
#   The `bgp` block routes the config. `ze` picks its startup path from
#   ProbeConfigType (internal/component/config/probe.go): a config whose only
#   top-level block is `plugin` is ConfigTypeHub and runs
#   runOrchestratorWithData (cmd/ze/hub/main.go), whose own parser accepts
#   `external` alone and dies at "parse config: line 2: expected 'external',
#   got internal". That is a hub parse error which never reaches LoadConfig, so
#   it proves nothing about the validators. One more top-level block routes the
#   same bytes to runYANGConfig, which is the path this test exists to hold.
#
#   The config is a `tmpfs=` file rather than a `stdin=` block because this
#   suite's runner REWRITES a bare `-` argument into the path of the stdin file
#   (runOneCommand, internal/test/runner/parsing.go), so `exec=ze -` here
#   becomes `ze <path>` -- the bare-config invocation form that no longer
#   exists, and the daemon answers "unknown command: <path>" with its usage.
#   `ze start <file>` is the form that starts the daemon from a file, and both
#   commands below read the one file, which is what makes them a comparison.

tmpfs=invalid-validator.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	internal p1 {
		use no-such-plugin-exists
	}
}

bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote { ip 127.0.0.1; }
			local { ip 127.0.0.1; accept false; }
		}
		session {
			asn { local 65000; remote 65001; }
			router-id 1.2.3.4;
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze start invalid-validator.conf:exit=1:timeout=20s
expect=exit:code=1
expect=stderr:contains=config validation failed
expect=stderr:contains=plugin
expect=stderr:contains=no-such-plugin-exists
expect=stderr:contains=is not a registered internal plugin

# The same config through `ze config validate` refuses identically. Both
# callers run one walk over one section list, so an operator who checks a
# config before an upgrade learns exactly what the daemon will do with it.
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze config validate invalid-validator.conf:exit=1
expect=exit:code=1
expect=stdout:contains=is not a registered internal plugin
